{#
  node_plan_beat.xml.j2

  Purpose:
    Scene Beat Partitioner prompt. Instructs the planner to slice the immediate scene
    into actionable beats with strict physical entry/exit constraints. Each beat gets
    a word budget, specific entry state (where characters are / what has just happened)
    and exit state (what must be true when this beat ends). The raw PAD coordinates
    are also generated here (step 1 of the PAD Translation Pipeline).

    Note: the PAD Grounded Translation Pipeline's step 3 (LLM adaptation of the raw
    behavioral constraint string) uses a SEPARATE prompt: node_plan_beat_pad.xml.j2.
    This template handles steps 1–2 (beat partitioning + raw PAD coordinate generation).

  Jinja2 Variables:
    {{ scene_id }}              str  — The ID of the scene being partitioned.
    {{ scene_description }}     str  — The scene's description from SQLite.
    {{ scene_word_budget }}     int  — Total word budget for the scene.
    {{ beats_per_scene_min }}   int  — Minimum number of beats required (from config).
    {{ character_pad_states }}  str  — JSON of current CharacterEmotions PAD states from
                                       SQLite for all characters active in this scene.
                                       Format: {character_id: {pleasure, arousal, dominance}}.
    {{ ewma_alpha }}            float — EWMA alpha value for PAD acceleration (from config).
    {{ scene_entry_context }}   str  — What is physically true at planning start: the last
                                       committed beat's exit constraints when extending an
                                       open scene, else the prior scene's closing prose.
    {{ graphiti_facts }}        str  — Point-in-time temporal facts from Graphiti (locations,
                                       possessions, relationships). May be empty.
    {{ thread_statuses }}       str  — Open subplot threads with priorities. May be empty.

  Output expected from model:
    Beat partitions:
    - beats: list of {id, scene_id, description, word_budget, beat_index,
        entry_constraints (str), exit_constraints (str),
        raw_pad_targets: {character_id: {pleasure, arousal, dominance}},
        pad_region: str (e.g., "high_arousal_low_dominance"),
        status: "planned"}.
    Format: JSON object.
#}

<prompt>
  <system>
    You are a scene beat architect. Partition this scene into actionable beats with
    strict entry and exit constraints. Output valid JSON only.
  </system>

  <context>
    <scene id="{{ scene_id }}">{{ scene_description }}</scene>
    <word_budget>{{ scene_word_budget }}</word_budget>
    <min_beats>{{ beats_per_scene_min }}</min_beats>
    <entry_context>{{ scene_entry_context }}</entry_context>
    <world_state>
      <facts>{{ graphiti_facts }}</facts>
      <threads>{{ thread_statuses }}</threads>
    </world_state>
    <character_emotional_states>{{ character_pad_states }}</character_emotional_states>
    <ewma_alpha>{{ ewma_alpha }}</ewma_alpha>
  </context>

  <instruction>
    Partition the scene into at least {{ beats_per_scene_min }} beats. Output a JSON object with exactly one top-level key:

    "beats": a list of beat objects, each with these fields:
      "id": string (scene_id prefixed),
      "scene_id": string,
      "beat_index": integer (0-based),
      "description": string,
      "word_budget": integer (sum must equal {{ scene_word_budget }}),
      "entry_constraints": string,
      "exit_constraints": string,
      "raw_pad_targets": object mapping character_id to {"pleasure": float, "arousal": float, "dominance": float},
      "pad_region": string (e.g., "high_pleasure_high_arousal"),
      "status": "planned"

    The first beat's entry_constraints must match the entry_context exactly.
    Every beat must be physically possible given the world_state facts —
    characters and objects are where the facts say they are; do not schedule
    actions that contradict them. Beats may only advance threads listed in
    world_state.
  </instruction>
</prompt>
